course: dictionary
lesson: 95
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word add has more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one. In «Pair the numbers from both ends to add fast: 1 + 4 = 5», add means to put numbers together to find the total.
explain: In «Add soap and the grease slides away», add means to put one more thing in with others. The clue is soap: you put soap into the water.
explain: In «And bring a coat,» she added, add means to say something more. A person is speaking, and there are no numbers or things being put together, so the clues point to words.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. «Add soap» can become «put soap in», and that still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Add means to put numbers or things together, so the total gets bigger.
fact: In maths, add is the sign +: 1 + 4 = 5.
fact: Phrases with add include add up, add on, add to, and add up to, which means to make a total.
fact: In cooking and science, «add the powder» means put more powder in.
fact: When you talk or write, «she added» means she said or wrote something more.
exercise: «Add soap and the grease slides away.» What does add mean here? | Take away | Count all the numbers | Say something more | *Put one more thing in
exercise: «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts.» What does add mean here? | *Put the amounts together to find the total | Share them out equally | Take the amounts away
exercise: «And bring a coat,» she added. What does added mean? | Put soap in the water | *Said something more | Put numbers together
test: «Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air.» Does adds mean put more in, or count a total here? | Say something more | Count a total | *Put more in
test: Which phrase means to make a total? | Add on the lid | *Add up to | Add a coat
exam: «J, U and W were added to English only in the last thousand years.» Which meaning fits best? | Counted as numbers | *Put in as new letters | Said out loud
